WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:13.410
When you’re planning your data project,
try to construct questions that will allow

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.650
you to leverage the data you currently have.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:21.150
It would be optimal to solve the biggest problem
your firm faces, and that should be the initial

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.550
goal.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:26.160
But if you don’t have the data to answer
that question, you’ll have difficulty trying

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.890
to do so.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:31.750
Consider other factors that could make your
question unsolvable.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.510
The source of the data, or the capability
of your team.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:41.290
Once you’ve determined what data is at your
disposal, ask actionable and detailed questions.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:45.809
They must be detailed in order to provide
you and your data team a narrowed scope of

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:50.950
focus, and actionable so you can accomplish
specific results after running the analyses.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:56.200
It’s helpful when your questions relate
to data that your business uses.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.699
Your questions must be aligned with strategic
goals, which vary depending on your target

00:01:00.699 --> 00:01:02.280
audience.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.840
Is this analysis for internal use, a C-Suite
meeting, a client?

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.340
Figure out your audience, and adjust your
question accordingly.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:16.030
Let’s imagine a scenario between two managers,
one who asks vague questions which will be

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:20.680
difficult to answer and one who asks direct
and actionable questions.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.390
Andreas: How can we cut costs across the retail
company?

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:30.320
Katie: Can we reduce cotton materials cost
by 20% and linen materials costs by 10% from

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.060
Supplier A?

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:36.250
Andreas’ question is too broad to design
a study.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:40.590
On the other hand, Katie’s question is detailed
and specific.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:44.979
To answer her question, we’ll need cost
data for supply chain and suppliers, and our

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:47.450
historical relationship with suppliers.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.579
Let’s take a look at the next set of questions.

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:55.430
Andreas: How can we increase our market share
in the US?

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:01.539
Katie: Which geographic market that we operate
in has the most potential for us to increase

00:02:01.539 --> 00:02:04.719
our market share?

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:09.500
Although both Andreas and Katie are focused
on market share, Katie’s question is framed

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.350
more specifically so we can identify the data
needed.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:17.450
For example, we’ll need both competitor
market shares and our market share for every

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.349
location where we have a store.

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:24.069
Let’s look at another example.

00:02:24.069 --> 00:02:27.250
Andreas: Should we expand internationally?

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:33.930
Katie: Could we capture 1% of the retail market
in Toronto if we open a store there?

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:38.540
Andreas is predictably asking broad questions
while Katie is focusing the analysis on a

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.930
specific goal and city.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:45.780
To answer her question, we’ll need competitor
data in Toronto, our success in comparable

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:52.019
markets, and customer surveys in Toronto asking
about brand awareness.

00:02:52.019 --> 00:02:53.909
We have one last question.

00:02:53.909 --> 00:02:57.430
Let’s see if Andreas has learned anything
from Katie.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:00.190
Andreas: Do we have enough employees?

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:04.950
Katie: Given the projects that we have scheduled
for next quarter, do we have enough skilled

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:09.730
personnel to fulfill our commitments?

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:14.170
Clearly Andreas hasn’t quite got the hang
of asking focused data questions.

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:19.409
His question’s still too vague and not actionable
enough for a data analysis.

00:03:19.409 --> 00:03:23.400
Katie’s question is well structured so it’s
clear we’ll need to know the size of the

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:29.049
projects, anticipated deadlines, required
skill sets, number of available employees,

00:03:29.049 --> 00:03:31.709
and current capacity.

00:03:31.709 --> 00:03:36.959
In summary, it might seem like a relatively
obvious point, but don’t underestimate the

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:41.400
importance of framing questions carefully
and thoughtfully before embarking on a data

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.110
analysis.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:47.510
This discipline will help guide your analysis
and ensure you derive meaningful information

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:48.239
from your data.

